+### Required failure behavior
+
+A skipped vector is a conformance-gate failure. Accepting an expected rejection,
+rejecting an expected positive case, substituting a local fixture, or omitting
+the real build-fixture exercise is also a failure.
+
+An unsupported schema, driver, receipt version, toolchain family, or policy
+must be rejected without fallback. Corrupt, noncanonical, linked, incomplete,
+or mismatched cache and marker inputs must not be executed or adopted. An
+unprovable protected-cache boundary is a miss for a real operation,
+`would-rebuild-untrusted-cache` for dry-run, and non-current for status.
+
+Failures before build eligibility run no source-aware Go command and cause no
+persistent mutation. A `go list` or `go build` failure discards private staging
+and preserves existing installations, consumers, and live cache state. Dry-run
+must not compile, link, repair, quarantine, publish, lock shared mutation state,
+or leave operation-private state behind. Status reports non-current without
+repairing it. Repair rebuilds from a revalidated snapshot; it never adopts
+self-consistent candidate bytes. These effects are part of the expected result,
+not optional diagnostics.
+
