+- [`build-receipt-v1.schema.json`](build-receipt-v1.schema.json) binds the
+  complete logical build input to the manager-derived artifact path, hash, and
+  size. Its exact canonical bytes and digest are consistency and currentness
+  identifiers, not independent provenance.
+- [`install-marker-v2.schema.json`](install-marker-v2.schema.json) records
+  `build_roots`, build-source identity, cache keys, receipt hashes, and
+  artifacts. Managers read markers v1 and v2 and write marker v2 after an
+  installation mutation. A valid marker v1 may remain current only for schema
+  1 through 5 packages.
+- [`conformance-claim-v2.schema.json`](conformance-claim-v2.schema.json) is the
+  claim for protocol `1.0.0-rc.4`. The historical
+  [`conformance-claim-v1.schema.json`](conformance-claim-v1.schema.json) remains
+  supported and frozen as rc.3 evidence; it cannot claim schema 6 or build
+  driver behavior.
+
+Schema examples and expected validation outcomes live in the
+[`schema-cases` index](../../conformance/v1/schema-cases/index.json). The exact
+compiled-build contracts are in
+[`build-drivers.json`](../../conformance/v1/vectors/build-drivers.json),
+[`manager-lifecycle.json`](../../conformance/v1/vectors/manager-lifecycle.json),
+and the generated
+[`build-driver` expected artifacts](../../conformance/v1/expected/build-driver/).
+
+## Standardizing a future driver
+
+A new language or toolchain is not added as a `go-v1` option or generic build
+fallback. Its proposal requires a new closed driver identifier, a closed
+manifest schema surface, and independent protocol and security threat review.
+The proposal must specify the complete compiler-input boundary; trusted
+toolchain identity; fixed direct process graph, argument vectors, environment,
+and child-process allowlist; network, dependency, linking, and output policy;
+logical cache-key and canonical receipt identity; dry-run, status, repair,
+rollback, and GC behavior; and manager-derived artifacts.
+
+Before standardization, the driver must add schema cases, a self-contained
+build fixture, generated expected identities, positive and rejection vectors,
+process and environment vectors, cache-corruption/currentness vectors, and
+manager lifecycle vectors. Its threat review and vectors must demonstrate that
+package-selected hooks, plugins, generators, macros, build recipes, arguments,
+environments, and produced output cannot execute during manager activity.

