# Curator CLI guide

This document is informative. It describes the Go reference implementation,
not a conformance requirement. Other managers may use different command names,
flags, output, machine-home paths, and distribution channels.

Curator uses `curator`, defaults its machine home to `~/.curator`, reads user
configuration from `~/.curator/config.json`, and supports overrides through
`CURATOR_CONFIG` and `CURATOR_SYSTEM_CONFIG`. Those names are not protocol wire
identifiers.

Protocol `1.0.0-rc.4` defines schema 6 and the `go-v1` build contract. This
guide explains how a Curator release that claims rc.4 manager conformance must
present that behavior; it does not assert that an already released Curator
binary supports schema 6. Check the binary's version and release notes before
using a schema 6 package. An older reader must diagnose the schema as
unsupported, not partially install it.

## Commands

| Command | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `curator bootstrap [--if-missing]` | Create machine configuration interactively or from flags; keep an existing file unchanged with `--if-missing` |
| `curator init [path]` | Create `Skillfile.json` and ignore generated paths |
| `curator add <name> --tag\|--branch\|--revision <ref>` | Add or replace a direct declaration and install |
| `curator remove <name>` | Remove a declaration |
| `curator install [target] [--all] [--dry-run] [--strict-tags] [--audit [advisory\|strict]]` | Apply the manager lifecycle |
| `curator update` | Fetch configured source repositories |
| `curator upgrade [target] [--all] [--dry-run]` | Fetch only the selected dependency closure, then install |
| `curator status [target] [--all] [--check] [--json] [--attest]` | Report drift and optionally refresh attestations |
| `curator list` | List configured projects and declarations |
| `curator project add\|resolve` | Register projects and resolve ownership of a path |
| `curator config show` | Print effective configuration |
| `curator skill check <dir> [--locale <code>] [--json]` | Validate one package |
| `curator global init\|add\|remove\|list\|status\|install\|update\|upgrade` | Manage global scope |
| `curator hybrid add\|remove\|list\|status` | Manage hybrid scope |
| `curator audit [target] [--all] [--global] [--json]` | Run source audit |
| `curator audit --allow <hash> --reason <text>` | Create an operator pin |
| `curator audit --publish <record> --registry <url>` | Publish an auditor-signed record |
| `curator gc` | Collect unreferenced machine state |
| `curator shell-init [auto\|zsh\|bash\|powershell] [--install] [--no-global]` | Print or cache optional shell integration |

Exit code 0 is success. Curator distinguishes usage errors, partial multi-
project failure, drift checks, and security-policy blocks with non-zero codes;
scripts should use `status --json` when they need structured details.

`bootstrap --if-missing` is intended for repository bootstrap commands. It
returns success without parsing or rewriting an existing configuration and is
mutually exclusive with `--force`.

`upgrade` differs from `update`: upgrade fetches only direct and transitive
sources required by the selected project or global manifest, while update
fetches every repository below `skills_root`. `upgrade --all` deduplicates
repositories shared by project closures. After fetching, upgrade applies the
same planning, cache, optional private-build, and serialized publication phases
as install.

## Schema 6 build behavior

The complete authoring example and schema links are in the
[`schemas/v1` index](../schemas/v1/README.md). A schema 6 build declaration has
one closed package-controlled shape:

```json
{
  "type": "build",
  "driver": "go-v1",
  "source_dir": "assets/build-tool/cmd/golden-tool"
}
```

The package also declares `build_roots`, such as
`["assets/build-tool"]`. The command name determines the output name; the
package does not choose it.

### Install and upgrade phase

For install and the install phase of upgrade, an rc.4-capable Curator validates
the whole immutable source snapshot, both possible manifest filenames,
runtime/build roots, source directories, closure, collisions, source policy,
audit, attestations, revocation, and moved tags before it resolves a toolchain,
opens a persistent build-cache candidate, or runs Go.

It then processes only active build commands in provider-first closure order
and command-name order. An exact protected cache hit runs no source-aware Go
command. On a miss, Curator runs the fixed `go list` preflight and then the
fixed `go build` into operation-private staging. All misses must build and
verify before shared installation state changes. Publication writes a complete
immutable cache entry and applies project, runtime, shim, adapter, and consumer
changes in one serialized journaled transaction. A failed preflight or build
discards private staging and leaves the existing installation, consumers, and
live cache unchanged.

### Fixed `go-v1` prerequisites

A schema 6 Go module must satisfy all of these constraints:

- `build_roots` names dedicated, real, link-free directories below the source
  snapshot. A root cannot be `.`, overlap a runtime root, or be unused.
- Each build root contains `go.mod` directly, and it is the nearest ancestor
  module for each command's `source_dir`. The source directory is a real,
  link-free directory below exactly one build root.
- The selected package is exactly one native package named `main`.
- Dependencies are complete under the module's checked-in `vendor/` tree.
  Network resolution, `go mod` mutation or download, workspaces, toolchain
  switching, and cross-compilation are unsupported.
- Curator selects an operator-trusted `<GOROOT>/bin/go` independently of the
  package. It accepts Go 1.23 or newer only when that release family has been
  tested by the manager against the `go-v1` vectors. Ordering a newer unknown
  family after a supported release is not enough.
- The fixed pipeline uses native `gc`, no PGO, `CGO_ENABLED=0`, internal
  linking, and no libgcc. Package-controlled assembly, host objects, C/C++,
  Objective-C, Fortran, SWIG, cgo directives, generators, tests, external
  linking, and helper processes are rejected.

The process graph contains only the package-independent telemetry, version,
and environment probes plus one `go list` and one `go build` for each cache
miss. Curator invokes the fingerprinted Go executable directly with the exact
protocol argument vectors and a closed, operation-private environment. It does
not use a shell.

There is deliberately no package argv, environment, flag, build tag,
toolchain, output, hook, plugin, generator, test, pre/post-build, or generic
build-system option in the manifest or CLI. Do not pass package arguments after
`--` or look for an escape-hatch flag: adding one would be a different driver
and protocol revision, not a `go-v1` option.

### Context and runtime exclusion

Every declared `build_roots` subtree is excluded from agent-facing context and
from the commit-keyed runtime copy. Curator computes that exclusion from the
validated manifest before locale rendering, cache lookup, or compiler
discovery, so a real build, protected cache hit, dry-run, and status check see
the same context boundary. A build root nested below an otherwise eligible
entry such as `assets/` remains excluded as a whole. Generated artifacts stay
only in manager-owned staging and immutable cache state; they are exposed
through command shims, never copied into prompt context.

### Cache, markers, and status

The logical build cache key binds the fully validated raw snapshot, command and
source selection, native target and tuning, exact trusted toolchain identity,
driver revision, and the complete fixed policy. Physical cache directory names
and receipt filenames are implementation details and must not be scripted.

Persistent reuse requires a manager-created, owner-protected, contained,
regular-file-only, link-safe boundary. Matching hashes outside that boundary do
not prove provenance. Curator rebuilds a real operation into new protected
state rather than changing permissions and adopting the candidate.

After any installation mutation, an rc.4 manager writes marker schema 2.
Marker v2 records build roots, raw-snapshot build-source identity, logical
cache keys, receipt hashes, artifact hashes, and manager-derived artifact
paths. Marker v1 can remain current only for an unchanged schema 1 through 5
installation; it cannot describe a schema 6 build.

`curator status` remains read-only. A build-enabled installation is current
only when all ordinary plan and installed-content checks pass and:

- build-root context and runtime exclusion are intact;
- the raw snapshot exists and its build-source identity still matches;
- target, toolchain, fixed policy, complete build input, and logical key match;
- the protected cache boundary is still provable; and
- the exact canonical receipt and regular artifact match their recorded path,
  hash, and size.

A missing raw snapshot, prompt-visible or runtime-copied build-root file,
unsupported driver or toolchain, untrusted cache boundary, corrupt receipt or
artifact, wrong native target, or source/key/path/hash mismatch is non-current.
`status --check` returns non-zero for that drift. Status does not execute the
artifact, quarantine cache state, or repair it; `status --json` is the
appropriate surface for automation to inspect the reported reason.

### Compiler-free dry-run

`install --dry-run` and `upgrade --dry-run` perform read-only planning and may
run only the package-independent `go telemetry off`, `go version`, and `go env`
probes. They do not run `go list`, `go build`, a compiler, or a linker. Per
active build command, the result is one of:

- `cache-hit` — the protected entry and exact receipt/artifact validate;
- `would-preflight-and-build` — a real operation would run `go list` and
  `go build` for a miss;
- `would-rebuild-untrusted-cache` — matching candidate bytes are outside a
  provable protected boundary;
- `corrupt` — a candidate receipt, artifact, or bound identity is malformed or
  inconsistent; or
- `unsupported` — the schema, driver, toolchain, target, or fixed policy cannot
  be implemented exactly.

A dry-run creates no persistent checkout, cache, lock, journal, marker, shim,
context, runtime, adapter, consumer, repair, quarantine, permission, GC, audit,
registry, or rollback state. Temporary probe state is removed before return.

### Diagnostics, repair, and GC

Diagnostics should identify the failing boundary and command without echoing
unbounded compiler output or treating untrusted paths as terminal control
data. Common categories are:

- unsupported reader, driver, target, or trusted Go release family;
- invalid or overlapping build roots, source directory, module, vendor tree,
  declaration field, or package graph;
- forbidden cgo, native input, directive, workspace, toolchain switch, helper,
  external-link, or libgcc behavior;
- corrupt or noncanonical receipt, wrong cache key/input/target/toolchain/
  policy/source, or artifact path/hash/size mismatch; and
- untrusted cache ownership, permissions, containment, file type, or link
  safety.

Unsupported and corrupt inputs fail closed. Curator does not fall back to a
system command, script command, shell hook, package build recipe, or another Go
toolchain. The built artifact is never run to diagnose, validate, version, or
repair it.

The protocol does not prescribe a standalone repair command name. When
Curator's documented reconciliation path repairs a build-enabled installation,
it must repeat snapshot validation, context exclusion, source and trust gates,
the fixed toolchain/process graph, private build, protected publication, and
journaled commit. Missing, corrupt, wrong-target, wrong-toolchain, or untrusted
entries are rebuilt; candidate bytes are never adopted by changing permissions
or rewriting only the marker.

`curator gc` serializes with shared manager-home mutation. For compiled state
it marks cache keys referenced by supported valid marker-v2 objects or in-flight
journals, then removes only unreferenced machine-local entries older than the
documented grace period. A receipt alone is not a live reference. Unreadable
markers/journals or an unprovable cache boundary fail safe by retaining or
conservatively quarantining uncertain entries and reporting the uncertainty.
GC never executes or adopts cache content. A post-commit GC failure is a
maintenance warning and does not roll back a successful installation.

### Compiler-input security limits

Go source, filenames, embedded data, compiler diagnostics, receipts, and cache
bytes remain untrusted. The fixed pipeline closes standard input, gives child
processes read-only source, uses private writable roots, denies network and
ambient executable search, bounds the output artifact, and allows only the
fingerprinted Go executable and its regular tool children. Curator bounds and
redacts diagnostic output and applies filesystem/network denial plus time,
memory, disk, process-count, and output limits where the host supports them.
If it cannot enforce the fixed environment, source/context separation, native
target, network denial, or process graph, it fails the build rather than
approximating the contract.

Compile-only does not make the compiler or artifact safe. The artifact remains
untrusted package code until a user later invokes the installed shim under the
consumer's own authorization and isolation policy.

## Developer shell

Shell profile setup is optional. Agent instructions can invoke project
commands directly through `.agents/bin/<command>` on Unix or
`.agents\bin\<command>.cmd` on Windows. Global installation publishes
forwarding shims to a safe existing user-bin directory when possible.
Set `CURATOR_GLOBAL_USER_BIN` to a writable directory already on `PATH` when
automatic selection is unavailable.

